 986 | Functions of an operating system | 2025-11-06 11:18:56.061776 | t               | batch_lesson_4_1_2_1_1762382644 | Functions of an operating system | Operating Systems and File Management | OS Foundations: Functions and Software Types |               45 |                   |                |               | <div><ul><li>Define an operating system and explain why a device cannot function without it.</li><li>List and describe core functions of an OS: user interface, process management, memory management, file management, device/I-O management, security, job scheduling/multitasking, error handling, networking, performance monitoring, and utilities.</li><li>Match real-life problems (e.g., slow system, printer not responding) to the OS functions that solve them.</li><li>Follow step-by-step procedures to perform basic OS tasks (view processes, manage files, connect to Wi‑Fi, create user accounts, check OS version, perform backups).</li><li>Evaluate how OS functions support Nigerian contexts like CBT centres, banks, media studios, and small businesses.</li></ul></div>                                 | <div><h4>Key Terms</h4><ul><li>Operating System: Master software managing hardware and software.</li><li>Process: A running program instance.</li><li>Driver: Software allowing OS to control a device.</li><li>Kernel: Core part of the OS managing resources.</li><li>Virtual Memory: Disk used to extend RAM.</li></ul><h4>Step-by-Step Procedures</h4><p><b>1) Check OS Version</b></p><ol><li>Windows: Press Windows key + R, type winver, press Enter. Read version.</li><li>Android: Settings > About phone > Software information.</li><li>Linux: Open Terminal, type lsb_release -a (or cat /etc/os-release).</li></ol><p><b>2) End a Frozen App Safely</b></p><ol><li>Windows: Press Ctrl+Shift+Esc to open Task Manager.</li><li>Select the app under “Processes.”</li><li>Click “End task.” Save work next time to avoid data loss.</li><li>Android: Settings > Apps > Select app > Force stop.</li></ol><p><b>3) Create a User Account (Windows)</b></p><ol><li>Open Settings > Accounts.</li><li>Select Family & other users.</li><li>Click “Add account” and choose local account if offline.</li><li>Set username and strong password/PIN.</li></ol><p><b>4) Connect to Wi‑Fi</b></p><ol><li>Turn on Wi‑Fi in Settings/Taskbar.</li><li>Select the correct SSID (network name).</li><li>Enter password; wait for “Connected.”</li><li>Test with a browser.</li></ol><p><b>5) Organize and Back Up Files</b></p><ol><li>Create a main folder (e.g., SS1_Computer_Studies).</li><li>Create subfolders by topic/date.</li><li>Copy important files to a flash drive or cloud.</li><li>Verify files open from the backup.</li></ol><p><b>6) Install a Printer (Windows)</b></p><ol><li>Connect printer via USB/Wi‑Fi.</li><li>Settings > Bluetooth & devices > Printers & scanners.</li><li>Click “Add device” and follow prompts.</li><li>Print a test page.</li></ol></div>                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | <div><ul><li>CBT Centre in Abuja: OS manages user logins, locks down browsing, schedules processes to keep systems responsive during peak exams.</li><li>Small Printing Shop in Ibadan: OS spools multiple print jobs, manages printer drivers, and handles file sharing over a local network.</li><li>Fintech App in Lagos: Uses OS security (biometrics) and networking to process transactions securely.</li><li>Media Studio in Enugu: OS handles large audio/video files, ensures real-time device input through drivers and prioritization.</li><li>Agritech Data Collection in Kaduna: Field tablets rely on OS power management, offline file storage, and secure syncing when network is available.</li></ul></div>                                                                               | <div><h4>Activity 1: Explore Task Manager (10–12 min)</h4><ol><li>Open Task Manager (Ctrl+Shift+Esc).</li><li>Identify running apps vs background processes.</li><li>Observe CPU and Memory columns; note the top consumers.</li></ol><h4>Activity 2: Memory Management Game (8–10 min)</h4><p>Use labeled cards as ‘apps’ and desks as ‘RAM slots.’ Students simulate loading/unloading apps; when RAM is full, move least-used card to ‘virtual memory’ (a labeled box).</p><h4>Activity 3: File Race (10–12 min)</h4><ol><li>Create a folder named SS1_OS_Functions.</li><li>Add subfolders: UI, Processes, Memory, Files, Devices, Security, Networking.</li><li>Place at least one sample file in each folder and back up to flash drive.</li></ol><h4>Activity 4: Device Check (8–10 min)</h4><p>Open Device Manager (Windows) or Settings > About phone > Status (Android). Identify at least two device drivers and check for warnings.</p><h4>Activity 5: Secure Me (8–10 min)</h4><ol><li>Create a new local user with a strong password.</li><li>Discuss why admin rights should be limited.</li></ol></div> | [                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 +| <div><ul><li><b>Home Survey:</b> Identify the OS on two devices at home (phone, laptop, smart TV). Write down version numbers and one OS function you used today.</li><li><b>Mini-Project:</b> Create a folder structure for your subjects. Save one sample file per subject and back up to a flash drive or cloud. Submit a screenshot of the structure.</li><li><b>Reflection Journal:</b> Describe a time your phone or computer ‘hung’. Which OS functions helped you recover?</li></ul></div>                                                                  | intermediate     | standard    | 2025-11-06 11:18:56.061789 | gpt-5         |       13127 |    1 |    2 |     1 | <div><h3>Imagine this at a Lagos bus park…</h3><p>You arrive at Ojota early for a class trip. Buses are honking, passengers are shouting, hawkers are selling gala. It looks chaotic—until you notice one person making everything work: the park manager. They assign buses, collect tickets, settle arguments, and make sure everyone gets to the right destination.</p><p>Your computer or phone looks calm on the surface too. But inside, there’s its own “park manager” working every second. That manager is the Operating System (OS). Without it, your device would be noisy metal and plastic doing nothing.</p><p>Today, you’ll meet the OS and learn its superpowers—how it controls apps, memory, files, security, devices, and more. After this lesson, every time your phone “hangs” or your laptop prints smoothly, you’ll know exactly which OS function is at work.</p><p>[Image: A busy motor park with a confident manager directing buses and people, alongside a simplified diagram of a computer with the OS at the center connecting apps and hardware]</p></div> | <div><h4>Example 1: System Is Slow with Many Apps Open</h4><p>Problem: Multiple apps open; laptop becomes sluggish.</p><p>OS Functions at Work: Process management, memory management, performance monitoring.</p><p>Solution Steps:</p><ol><li>Open Task Manager; sort by Memory or CPU.</li><li>Close unnecessary apps normally; if frozen, end task.</li><li>Check startup apps and disable non-essential ones.</li><li>Ensure enough free disk space for virtual memory.</li></ol><h4>Example 2: Printer Not Responding in a Business Centre</h4><p>Problem: Jobs pile in the queue but nothing prints.</p><p>OS Functions: Device management, spooling, error handling.</p><p>Solution Steps:</p><ol><li>Open Printers & Scanners; check printer status.</li><li>Clear jammed queue; restart Print Spooler service.</li><li>Verify driver installed; reinstall if corrupt.</li><li>Print a test page.</li></ol><h4>Example 3: “Access Denied” When Saving to a Folder</h4><p>Problem: Cannot save into C:\Program Files.</p><p>OS Functions: Security and permissions.</p><p>Solution Steps:</p><ol><li>Save to Documents or Desktop (user-writable).</li><li>If needed, run app as admin temporarily (with permission).</li><li>Adjust folder permissions only if you are authorized.</li></ol><h4>Example 4: Wi‑Fi Connects but No Internet</h4><p>Problem: Connected to Wi‑Fi, but pages don’t load.</p><p>OS Functions: Networking, error handling.</p><p>Solution Steps:</p><ol><li>Run Network Troubleshooter (Windows) or toggle Airplane mode (Android).</li><li>Check IP settings (obtain automatically via DHCP).</li><li>Forget and reconnect to network.</li><li>Restart router (if permitted) or try another network.</li></ol></div> | <div><ul><li>Which OS function protects files from unauthorized access?</li><li>If an app freezes, which two OS functions are most involved in resolving it?</li><li>Why does the OS use virtual memory, and where does it store it?</li><li>What role do drivers play in printing a document?</li><li>How does the OS create the feeling that many apps run at once?</li></ul></div>                      | <div><ul><li>I can clearly explain the difference between an OS and an application.</li><li>When my system is slow, I know which OS tools to check first and why.</li><li>I can connect at least three OS functions to real situations in Nigeria.</li><li>I feel confident creating user accounts and organizing my files.</li></ul></div>               | <div><p>An Operating System (OS) is the master program that controls everything your computer or phone does. It starts when the device powers on, wakes up the hardware, provides a way for you to interact (touch, mouse, voice), and ensures apps share resources safely and efficiently. Popular examples include Windows, Android, Linux, and iOS. In WAEC/NECO, you must explain what the OS does—its core functions—and give real examples.</p><p>Think of the OS as: manager of programs, guardian of security, keeper of files, controller of devices, and communicator on networks. In this section, you will learn each function with practical steps and Nigerian examples—CBT centres, printing shops, fintech apps, and more.</p></div>                                                                                                                                                                                           | <div><h3>1. What Is an Operating System?</h3><p>An Operating System (OS) is system software that manages computer hardware and software resources, and provides common services for computer programs. Without an OS, a computer cannot run user applications.</p><p>[Image: Layered diagram showing User -> Applications -> Operating System (Kernel + Services) -> Hardware]</p><p>Examples: Microsoft Windows, Linux (Ubuntu), Android, iOS, macOS.</p><hr/><h3>2. Core Functions of an Operating System</h3><h4>2.1 User Interface (UI)</h4><p>The OS provides how you interact with the system—Graphical User Interface (GUI) with icons, menus, touch; or Command Line Interface (CLI) with typed commands.</p><ul><li>GUI example: Windows desktop, Android home screen.</li><li>CLI example: Command Prompt/PowerShell (Windows), Terminal (Linux).</li></ul><p>Why it matters: Makes the system usable for different users and tasks.</p><h4>2.2 Process Management</h4><p>Programs (apps) are made of processes. The OS starts, pauses, resumes, and ends processes; shares CPU time; prevents conflicts; and handles “frozen” apps.</p><ul><li>Example: Using Task Manager to end a non-responsive app in Windows; Force stop on Android.</li></ul><p>[Image: CPU time slices allocated to multiple processes]</p><h4>2.3 Memory Management</h4><p>The OS allocates RAM to processes, keeps active data close for speed, uses virtual memory (swap/page file) when RAM is low, and prevents one app from reading another app’s memory.</p><ul><li>Example: Many tabs open in a browser; the OS moves some data to disk temporarily.</li></ul><h4>2.4 File and Folder Management</h4><p>The OS stores data as files inside folders on storage devices (HDD, SSD, flash). It handles file naming, saving, relocating, searching, permissions, and backups.</p><ul><li>Example: WAEC past questions saved in Documents; OS maintains file system structure (e.g., NTFS, ext4).</li></ul><p>[Image: Tree diagram of folders and files]</p><h4>2.5 Device and I/O Management</h4><p>The OS communicates with hardware through drivers (printers, keyboards, network cards). It manages input/output (I/O) queues, buffering, and device permissions.</p><ul><li>Example: Plug-and-play flash drive appears automatically; printer driver enables printing.</li></ul><h4>2.6 Security and Access Control</h4><p>The OS protects the system with user accounts, passwords/PINs, permissions, encryption, and audit logs. It separates standard users from administrators.</p><ul><li>Example: School lab students log in with limited rights; only admin can install software.</li></ul><h4>2.7 Job Scheduling and Multitasking</h4><p>The OS schedules CPU tasks to give the illusion of many programs running at once. It prioritizes critical tasks and background services.</p><ul><li>Example: You listen to music while downloading files and typing notes—OS time-sharing makes it smooth.</li></ul><h4>2.8 Error Detection and Interrupt Handling</h4><p>The OS detects errors (e.g., device failure, memory access error) and responds to interrupts from hardware or software (e.g., a key press, printer ready signal).</p><ul><li>Example: “Program not responding” alert; safe restart after a crash.</li></ul><h4>2.9 Networking</h4><p>The OS configures network settings, manages Wi‑Fi/Ethernet, applies firewalls, and supports protocols (TCP/IP) for sharing data and resources.</p><ul><li>Example: Connecting to school Wi‑Fi, file sharing on a LAN in a business centre.</li></ul><h4>2.10 System Performance and Utilities</h4><p>The OS monitors CPU, memory, disk, and network usage; provides utilities like Disk Cleanup, Update Manager, Backup/Restore, and Accessibility features.</p><ul><li>Example: Using Windows Update; Android battery optimization settings.</li></ul><h4>2.11 Application Support and APIs</h4><p>The OS provides Application Programming Interfaces (APIs) that apps use to access hardware safely (camera, GPS, file system) without damaging the system.</p><ul><li>Example: A fintech app uses Android APIs for fingerprint authentication.</li></ul><hr/><h3>3. OS Functions in Daily Life</h3><ul><li>CBT centres: user accounts, timed exams, locked-down browsers—security and process control.</li><li>Printing shops: driver management and spooling for multiple print jobs.</li><li>Mobile banking: networking, security (encryption/biometrics), and app sandboxing.</li><li>Music/film studios: large file handling, device driver support for audio interfaces.</li></ul><hr/><h3>4. Case Study: Power Interruption in a Cybercafé</h3><p>During a sudden power cut, systems restart on generator. The OS performs filesystem checks, resumes network connections, and recovers unsaved files from autosave. Journaling file systems (e.g., NTFS) reduce corruption. UPS plus OS shutdown signals protect data.</p><hr/><h3>5. Vocabulary</h3><ul><li><b>Process:</b> A running program instance.</li><li><b>Thread:</b> A lightweight unit of execution inside a process.</li><li><b>Driver:</b> Software that lets the OS talk to hardware.</li><li><b>Kernel:</b> Core of the OS managing resources.</li><li><b>Virtual memory:</b> Disk space used to extend RAM.</li><li><b>File system:</b> Method the OS uses to store and organize files.</li></ul></div>                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |                          | <div class="teacher-notes"><h4>🎯 Teaching Strategy</h4><p>Use the motor park analogy to bridge abstract OS concepts with a familiar Nigerian scene. Ask: “Who has been to Ojota or Jabi park?” Invite quick stories. Then connect the park manager role to the OS: assigning resources, resolving conflicts, keeping order.</p><h4>💡 Practical Examples</h4><ul><li>Show your phone’s app switcher: explain the OS keeps multiple apps ready like the manager keeps multiple buses moving.</li><li>Open Task Manager (Windows) or Settings > Apps (Android) briefly to visualize the OS managing processes.</li></ul><h4>⏰ Timing Guide</h4><ul><li>Story and analogy: 2 minutes</li><li>Student sharing: 2 minutes</li><li>OS connection statement: 1 minute</li></ul><h4>🎓 Differentiation</h4><p>For students with limited exposure to motor parks, use a school prefect coordinating assembly as an alternative analogy.</p><h4>🎪 Student Engagement</h4><p>Ask students to mime a “hung” app and another student acting as the OS resolving it—light humor builds comfort.</p><h4>⚠️ Common Misconceptions</h4><p>Students may think the OS is just the look (icons, wallpaper). Emphasize that the user interface is only one function; deeper functions run silently.</p><h4>✅ Assessment Cues</h4><p>Look for students who can articulate at least two things the OS ‘manages’ after the hook.</p><h4>🔧 Resources Needed</h4><p>Phone or computer to demo app switching or Task Manager; projector if available.</p></div>                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | <div class="teacher-notes"><h4>🎯 Concept Introduction</h4><p>Connect to prior knowledge: ask students to name the OS on their phone/computer. Write a quick list on the board (Android, Windows, iOS, Linux/Ubuntu). Clarify that while they look different, they perform the same core functions.</p><h4>💡 Practical Examples</h4><ul><li>Show how the OS boots: play a short boot video (if available) or describe “Power on -> Bootloader -> Kernel -> Log-in screen.”</li><li>Explain CBT centre operations (JAMB): scheduling, user accounts, security—these are OS functions in the background.</li></ul><h4>⏰ Timing Guide</h4><ul><li>Prior knowledge activation: 3 minutes</li><li>Definition & purpose: 4 minutes</li><li>Preview of functions: 3 minutes</li></ul><h4>🎓 Differentiation</h4><p>For students unfamiliar with desktops, relate to Android Settings and app permissions. For advanced students, mention the kernel and APIs briefly.</p><h4>⚠️ Misconceptions</h4><p>Students may confuse OS with application software. Emphasize: apps need the OS to run; the OS does not need any single app to exist.</p><h4>✅ Assessment Cues</h4><p>By the end of the intro, students should describe the OS as a ‘manager’ and name at least three functions.</p></div>                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | <div class="teacher-notes"><h4>Communicating Objectives</h4><p>Display the objectives and read them aloud. Ask students to pick one personal goal (e.g., “learn to stop a frozen app safely”). Link objectives to exam expectations: “WAEC/NECO may ask you to state and explain functions of an OS with examples.”</p><h4>Tracking Progress</h4><ul><li>Use a checklist: after each function is taught, students tick off understanding and add an example.</li><li>Exit ticket: write one OS function and a matching real-life scenario.</li></ul><h4>Assessment</h4><p>Formative questioning after each function; practical demonstrations in the lab; quick pair-share explanations to test verbal understanding.</p><h4>Ownership</h4><p>Invite students to suggest the OS tasks they most want to master (e.g., file backups, permissions). Promise to cover them in practicals.</p></div>                                                                                                                                                     | <div class="teacher-notes"><h4>Overall Pacing (40–50 minutes)</h4><ul><li>What is an OS (definition + diagram): 5 minutes</li><li>Core functions (2.1–2.11): 25 minutes with mini-demos and quick checks</li><li>Daily life + case study: 5 minutes</li><li>Vocabulary consolidation: 3 minutes</li><li>Q&A and transition to activities: 5–10 minutes</li></ul><h4>Explanation Strategies</h4><ul><li>Use layered visuals: User -> Apps -> OS -> Hardware. Reinforce that the OS is between applications and hardware.</li><li>For each function, give a simple sentence: “The OS does X so that Y works.” Add a Nigerian example.</li><li>Alternate speaking with quick demos: open Task Manager, show Wi‑Fi settings, display File Explorer, open Printer settings.</li></ul><h4>Differentiation</h4><ul><li>Struggling learners: Provide a one-page “Function and Example” handout; pair with peers in demonstrations.</li><li>Advanced learners: Ask them to explain how virtual memory works or why drivers must match hardware.</li></ul><h4>Engagement</h4><ul><li>Use call-and-response: “Who manages processes?” students respond “The OS!”</li><li>Relate each function to a school scenario: exams, projects, class WhatsApp groups.</li></ul><h4>Common Challenges</h4><ul><li>Confusing application updates with OS updates: clarify both exist and affect different layers.</li><li>Thinking security is only antivirus: stress accounts, permissions, encryption, and updates are OS-level too.</li></ul><h4>Assessment During Lesson</h4><ul><li>Ask students to identify which OS function applies to a scenario (e.g., “Printer queue jam”).</li><li>Observe students performing a quick action: ending a frozen app, finding OS version.</li></ul><h4>Resources</h4><p>Windows or Linux PCs/Android phones, projector, whiteboard, internet optional.</p></div>                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | <div class="teacher-notes"><h4>Instructional Strategy</h4><p>Model each procedure live if possible. After demonstration, give students a printed/posted checklist to follow independently. Emphasize safe actions (e.g., saving work before ending tasks).</p><h4>Memory Aids</h4><p>For Wi‑Fi: “N-PAT” – Network on, Pick SSID, Add password, Test connection. For backups: “3-2-1 Rule” (3 copies, 2 locations, 1 off-site if possible).</p><h4>Ensuring Understanding</h4><p>Circulate during practice. Ask students to explain in their own words why the OS is needed for each procedure (e.g., drivers for devices, permissions for accounts).</p></div>                                                                                                                                                                                 | <div class="teacher-notes"><h4>Facilitating Connections</h4><p>Ask students to pick one scenario they have seen (e.g., printing shop). In pairs, list which OS functions are involved. Share out. Encourage students to add one new scenario from their community (church/mosque media team, POS operators).</p><h4>Career Relevance</h4><p>Highlight roles: System administrator, Network technician, Cybersecurity analyst, Support technician. Each relies on understanding OS functions.</p></div>                                                                                                                                     | <div class="teacher-notes"><h4>Activity Management</h4><p>Divide the class into small groups. Assign roles: Reader (reads steps), Doer (operates the computer), Recorder (notes observations), Reporter (shares with class). Rotate roles across activities.</p><h4>Adaptations</h4><p>Limited PCs: Run stations; groups rotate every 8–10 minutes. No PCs: Use simulation with cards and posters; demonstrate steps on a projector from teacher’s laptop.</p><h4>Maximizing Learning</h4><p>Require each group to produce evidence (screenshot notes, folder tree map, list of top processes). Use a rubric: completeness, accuracy, teamwork.</p><h4>Safety and Policy</h4><p>Remind students not to end system processes they don’t recognize. Ensure no unauthorized software installation. For shared labs, revert settings after activities.</p></div> | <div class="teacher-notes"><h4>Modeling Thinking</h4><p>Verbalize your reasoning: “I suspect memory pressure because CPU is fine but RAM is high.” Show how to interpret Task Manager graphs.</p><h4>Participation</h4><p>Ask students to predict the next step before you do it. Use think-pair-share: give the scenario, let pairs propose a solution path, then demonstrate.</p><h4>Checking Understanding</h4><p>Have students map each step to an OS function (e.g., clearing spooler → device/I‑O management).</p></div>                                                                         | <div class="teacher-notes"><h4>When to Use</h4><p>Insert after explaining 2–3 functions. Use quick hands-up, mini-whiteboards, or thumbs-up/down to gauge understanding.</p><h4>Feedback</h4><p>Clarify immediately: e.g., “Drivers are not apps for users; they are system-level interpreters between OS and devices.” Encourage students to justify their answers.</p></div>                                                                                                                                               | <div class="teacher-notes"><h4>Administration & Marking</h4><p>Mix MCQs with application scenarios. Award marks for both function identification and practical steps. Encourage clear linking of problems to OS functions.</p><h4>Common Errors</h4><ul><li>Listing apps instead of OS functions.</li><li>Confusing security with antivirus only.</li><li>Ignoring drivers in device problems.</li></ul><h4>Feedback</h4><p>Return papers with model answers and a mapping guide (Problem → Function → Steps). Ask students to correct one answer in a different color.</p></div>                                                                                                                                                                                             | <div class="teacher-notes"><h4>Explanation & Differentiation</h4><p>Provide a sample screenshot of a neat folder tree. For students without home devices, allow them to complete during library/lab time or write the steps theoretically.</p><h4>Feedback</h4><p>Give short, specific comments: organization, correct version reporting, realistic reflections.</p></div>                                                                                                                                                           | <div class="teacher-notes"><h4>Facilitation</h4><p>Use a quick exit slip with the prompts rated 1–5. Discuss common trends next class and plan a short revision where needed (e.g., permissions).</p></div>                                                                                                                                          | <div><h4>Quick Summary: Functions of an OS</h4><ul><li>UI: Lets you interact (GUI/CLI).</li><li>Process Management: Starts/stops apps and shares CPU time.</li><li>Memory Management: Allocates RAM and uses virtual memory.</li><li>File Management: Saves, organizes, secures, and backs up files.</li><li>Device/I-O Management: Uses drivers to control hardware.</li><li>Security: Accounts, passwords, permissions, encryption.</li><li>Job Scheduling/Multitasking: Runs many tasks smoothly.</li><li>Error/Interrupt Handling: Detects problems and responds quickly.</li><li>Networking: Connects to Wi‑Fi/Ethernet, shares resources, firewall.</li><li>Performance/Utilities: Monitors system, updates, cleanup, backup.</li><li>Application Support/APIs: Safe access to hardware features.</li></ul><h4>Mnemonic</h4><p>Jollof Every Meal Feeds Us Nigerians During Parties (J E M F U N D S P)</p><ul><li>J – Job scheduling</li><li>E – Error handling</li><li>M – Memory management</li><li>F – File management</li><li>U – User interface</li><li>N – Networking</li><li>D – Device management</li><li>S – Security</li><li>P – Performance/utilities</li></ul><h4>Exam Tips</h4><ul><li>Always define the function + give a Nigerian example.</li><li>Differentiate OS vs application software.</li><li>Use clear keywords: allocate, schedule, driver, permission, process.</li></ul></div>                                                                  | ed4176bd-bbc9-4279-9d28-5d8311db05fa
     |                                  |                            |                 |                                 |                                  |                                       |                                              |                  |                   |                |               |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 